1
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:09,060
A school has 1,200 students.

2
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:14,950
The head wants to know: do students who use phones and screens more, sleep less?

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,440
Asking everyone would take far too long.

4
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:24,520
What is the best first step: write a clear question and predictions, or ask your friends?

5
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:27,760
Pause the video and choose.

6
00:00:27,810 --> 00:00:30,920
Write a clear question and predictions first.

7
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:34,340
Then you know exactly what data you need.

8
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:37,950
Remember: primary data is data you collect yourself.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,200
Secondary data was collected by someone else, like data from a government website.

10
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:48,900
First, write a statistical question, and a prediction you can test.

11
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:55,330
For example: students with more than 3 hours of screen time sleep less than 8 hours.

12
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:58,850
Then decide what data to collect, how, and how much.

13
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:04,620
Collect it, show it, analyse it, and say whether it supports your prediction.

14
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:05,300
Your turn.

15
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:09,770
A prediction you can test: is it called a hypothesis, or a sample?

16
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:13,000
Pause the video and choose.

17
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:15,310
It is a hypothesis.

18
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:18,680
The population is everyone you want to know about.

19
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:21,250
A sample is the part you actually ask.

20
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:24,370
Here are four ways to choose a sample.

21
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:30,360
Stratified sampling takes each group, like each year group, in proportion to its size.

22
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:36,060
Convenience sampling, like asking your friends, is quick but often unfair.

23
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:40,880
A bigger sample is usually more reliable, but takes longer.

24
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:45,350
For 1,200 students, about 60 to 120 is sensible.

25
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:46,710
Here is the method.

26
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:50,700
Step 1: write the question and your predictions.

27
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:55,140
Step 2: decide what data to collect, from whom, and how.

28
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:59,750
Step 3: choose a fair sampling method and a sensible size.

29
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:04,820
Step 4: plan how to show and analyse the data, then interpret it.

30
00:02:04,870 --> 00:02:11,430
A school has 400 students in Year 7, 300 in Year 8 and 500 in Year 9.

31
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:15,460
They choose a sample of 60, in proportion.

32
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:17,470
How many from Year 9?

33
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:21,280
500 out of 1,200, times 60, is 25.

34
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:22,010
Your turn.

35
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:28,470
Choosing every 20th name on a list: is that systematic sampling, or stratified sampling?

36
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,110
Pause and choose.

37
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:33,480
It is systematic sampling.

38
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:38,840
To choose 50 from 1,200, divide: 1,200 divided by 50 is 24.

39
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:43,650
Pick a random start, then take every 24th name.

40
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:45,420
Good questions are clear.

41
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:47,750
Not: how much do you use screens?

42
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,700
But: yesterday, how many hours did you spend on screens?

43
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:55,710
With answer boxes like 0 to 1, 1 to 2, and so on.

44
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:59,820
Ben wants the favourite sport of all 1,200 students.

45
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:01,960
Which line is wrong?

46
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:03,040
Line 1 is wrong.

47
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:08,350
The sample is far too small, and it is biased: they all play football.

48
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:15,090
Use a larger random or stratified sample, like 100 students from the whole school.

49
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:21,370
Opinion polls often ask about 1,000 people to estimate what millions of people think.

50
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:25,830
It works because the sample is chosen very carefully.

51
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:27,210
One last question.

52
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:29,360
A school has 1,200 students.

53
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:31,280
The sample is 10 percent.

54
00:03:31,330 --> 00:03:34,050
How many students are in the sample?

55
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:37,520
Pause the video and answer.

56
00:03:37,570 --> 00:03:41,640
10 percent of 1,200 is 120.

57
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:46,570
To remember: question, predictions, data, analysis, conclusion.

58
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:48,450
The population is everyone.

59
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:50,800
The sample is who you ask.

60
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:54,080
Use random, systematic or stratified samples.

61
00:03:54,130 --> 00:03:55,940
Avoid convenience.

62
00:03:55,990 --> 00:03:59,360
Bigger samples are usually more reliable.

63
00:03:59,410 --> 00:04:04,550
Great work! Now practise lesson 6.1 on Camday.
